# Maewyn confronts the player the moment her cellar lock is picked while she
# is in the party (opened directly by the pick job - no walk-up).
# Compile with:  scripts\Compile-Dialogue.ps1

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_Maewyn_CellarTheft

== start ==
The lock has barely finished yielding when you feel it: the silence of being watched by a druid and her raven.

"I know my own lock's voice, rider."

Maewyn doesn't raise hers. She doesn't need to. "Thirty years, that lock. It has kept out storms, wolves, and two kings' tax collectors." Corvus clicks his beak once, like a gavel. "It couldn't keep out my own company."

* "I should have asked. I'm sorry, Maewyn." -> sorry
    do flag(TSC_MaewynCellarAired)
* "You were never going to just open it for me." -> defiant
    do flag(TSC_MaewynCellarAired); affinity(-5)
* [Say nothing.] -> silence
    do flag(TSC_MaewynCellarAired)

== sorry ==
She looks at you for a long moment. Some of the frost goes out of it. Not all.

"The book you may keep; knowledge should be shared, and you could have just asked. The trust will take longer to restock than the shelf."

* "It won't happen again." -> end

== defiant ==
"For the company's good, was it." Her voice is sharp as winter.

"Then the company can carry its own opinion of it. Keep the book. My locks are done being polite about the rest."

* "Understood." -> end

== silence ==
The silence stretches, and she lets it. Corvus does the judging for both of them.

"The book you may keep. Knowledge wants legs. Trust, rider, walks slower."

* "..." -> end
